%I A401730 #17 Nov 09 2026 23:26:20
%S A401730 1,2,10,15,225,5005,25050025,4340324450
%N A401730 Numbers k with no p^p-factors, such that A083345(k) is a multiple of A276085(k), where A083345 is the numerator of the fully additive function with a(p) = 1/p, and A276085 is fully additive with a(p) = p#/p.
%C A401730 Equally, numbers k with no p^p-factors, such that A342001(k) is a multiple of A276085(k). This follows because when their domain is restricted to A048103, sequences A083345 and A342001 reduce to the same function. For example A083345(A276086(n)) = A342001(A276086(n)) = A342002(n).
%C A401730 As this is the sequence A276086(A401729(.)) sorted into ascending order, we know that also the following terms are present: 125375375125, 50726876775575, 627503752500625, 2803555083732433030, 48461452161660628090, 837690815937276571270, 14480084104058637874810, 250298596655870740407430, 2840027504167937996637725, 135730131917554591054606843875, 216194668706246201133688649383063650.
%C A401730 Question: Where is the first difference from A401732? See comments there.
%o A401730 (PARI)
%o A401730 A083345(n) = { my(f=factor(n)); numerator(vecsum(vector(#f~, i, f[i, 2]/f[i, 1]))); };
%o A401730 A276085(n) = { my(f = factor(n)); sum(k=1, #f~, f[k, 2]*prod(i=1, primepi(f[k, 1]-1), prime(i))); };
%o A401730 A359550(n) = { my(pp); forprime(p=2, , pp = p^p; if(!(n%pp), return(0)); if(pp > n, return(1))); };
%o A401730 is_A401730(k) = ((1==k) || (A359550(k) && !(A083345(k)%A276085(k))));
%Y A401730 Intersection of A048103 and A401728. A401732 is a proper subsequence.
%Y A401730 Cf. A083345, A276086, A276086, A342001, A342002, A401729.
%Y A401730 Cf. also A401726 (contains many of the same terms).
%K A401730 nonn,hard,more,new
%O A401730 1,2
%A A401730 _Antti Karttunen_, Nov 09 2026
